WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Today, and also at the time I was writing my dissertation, my main area of activity has been the study of discharge in insulating liquids

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Insulating liquids are used mainly in high-voltage transformers

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Obviously, in general, those transformers have liquid breakdowns.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Therefore, it is important to understand the discharges in order to understand the defects of these electrical apparatus

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Mathematica was already being used when I arrived at the lab.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:03.000
It was used by some researchers 15 or 20 years ago.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:13.000
Since then, we used Mathematica more and more, to the point where it became our main tool.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:20.000
We have experimental results that we are able to analyze very easily with Mathematica

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000
and eventually compare them to theories or models.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:32.000
The first two notebooks concern the discharge aspect in insulating liquids

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:50.000
I will need to explain (laughter).

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:58.000
We are trying to understand the application of electric fields, namely high electric fields, on insulating liquids

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.000
Mathematica is very interesting for analytical calculations

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Mathematica is very interesting for analytical calculations

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
And then, I would say that my biggest interest in Mathematica

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:17.000
is to be able to marry both the analytical calculation and numerical computation in a single formulation of code

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.000
and be able to mix it all just to get possibly a confrontation model showing discrepancies in experimental and theoretical results.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.000
And then the big advantage that makes things easier with Mathematica

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:37.000
is that from a number of data points we will be able to reconstruct

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:42.000
in fact, quite easily a continuous model from only a few points

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:49.000
since Mathematica has this ability to make functions from interpolations

00:02:49.000 --> 00:03:00.000
So, you can simplify the problem that was initially difficult or had only a few points tabulated.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.000
Somehow we can finally solve this continuous problem using this aspect of Mathematica.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:26.000
The goal of the third notebook was to show that we were able to somehow develop an experiment.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:38.000
The device we realized after this study--we used Mathematica in part of this study

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:48.000
is the realization of a calorimeter to measure the dielectric losses in the power of electronic components.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.000
The calorimetric device we designed was precisely to measure

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.000
the quantity of heat emitted by the components of dielectric power.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Which was not necessarily possible with any other calorimetric techniques available today

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Mathematica was used mainly for it, that is to say the device dimension

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.000
but also to find properties, good properties

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:18.000
and therefore the right materials that will allow us to make this device.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.000
One of the many advantages of Mathematica is the Manipulate function

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:31.000
that allows you to see in real time the influence of parameters

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:36.000
in an extremely fast and very visual way. It is fantastic.

